      "answer": "$c=4$, $P=(\\frac12,-1)$; or $c=-1$, $P=(-2,-11)$",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$cx^2+2x-3=6x-c$, leading to $cx^2-4x+(c-3)=0$ — B1. Three-term quadratic.\n$16-4c(c-3)=0$ — *M1. Apply $b^2-4ac=0$; $=0$ may be implied. Their coefficients must be substituted correctly.\n$4c^2-12c-16=0$, so $(c-4)(c+1)=0$, giving $c=4,-1$ — A1. Dependent on factorisation OE.\nFor $c=4$, $4x^2-4x+1=0$, or $(2x-1)^2=0$ — DM1. OE. Substituting their $c=4$.\n$x=\\frac12$, $y=-1$ — A1. Both required.\nFor $c=-1$, $x^2+4x+4=0$, or $(x+2)^2=0$ — DM1. OE. Substituting their $c=-1$.\n$x=-2$, $y=-11$ — A1. Both required.\nAlternative differentiation method: $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2cx+2$ — B1.\nAlternative differentiation method: $2cx+2=6$ — M1. Equate the curve gradient to 6.\nAlternative differentiation method: $c=\\frac2x$ — A1 SOI.\nAlternative differentiation method: $2x^2+3x-2=0$ — DM1. Substitute $c=\\frac2x$ into $cx^2+2x-3=6x-c$ and simplify to a three-term quadratic.\nAlternative differentiation method: $(2x-1)(x+2)=0$, so $x=\\frac12$ or $x=-2$ — A1. Dependent on factorisation; both required.\nAlternative differentiation method: $c=4$ and $c=-1$ — A1. Both required. If DM0, SC B1 for both.\nAlternative differentiation method: $y=-1$ and $y=-11$ — A1. Both required. If DM0, SC B1 for both. SC one correct $(x,y)$: A1 only.\nTotal: 7",
